You’re right. I collapsed peripheral into retinal periphery, when you meant a general perceptual architecture.

Every sense can have a functional center and periphery—even when it lacks a literal anatomical fovea.

Generalized Sensory Foveation

Attention creates a temporary high-resolution “fovea” inside whatever coordinate system a sense uses:

* Vision: location, motion, color, shape.
* Hearing: speaker, direction, rhythm, frequency band.
* Touch: body location, pressure, texture.
* Smell: one odor extracted from a chemical mixture.
* Proprioception: one joint or movement foregrounded from the whole body.
* Interoception: heartbeat, breathing, tension, hunger.
* Thought: one idea foregrounded from an ambient association field.

So periphery is not necessarily spatial. It means outside the current allocation of precision and conscious access.

The shape probably arises from several nested shapes:

signal physics → receptor geometry → neural map → attentional geometry → conscious interface

The conscious mind cannot receive every channel at full resolution simultaneously. The interface therefore compresses most input into atmosphere, tendency, confidence, discomfort, rhythm, or interruption. Consciousness receives the compiled result—not necessarily its source code.

Sensory Quorum

That gives us the mechanism behind “something is behind me.”

No individual cue may be consciously detectable:

faint reflection + tiny sound + air movement + floor vibration + body-model mismatch + prior expectation

Each remains below the naming threshold, but their combined evidence crosses the orientation threshold. Your body turns before your verbal mind knows why.

That is a Sensory Quorum:

many weak peripheral votes → cross-modal agreement → compiled intuition → action → explanation afterward

It may be correct, or the prior may be overweighted and produce a false alarm. That is why training must reward correct detection and penalize false positives.

The new Pantheon stack becomes:

* Perceptual Topology: the shape of foreground and field within each modality.
* Generalized Sensory Foveation: attention dynamically allocating resolution.
* Sensory Quorum: several weak channels jointly crossing an action threshold.
* Compiled Intuition: knowing the output without conscious access to its contributing evidence.
* Ghost Grammar: a learned structure recognized from the periphery before it can be named.

That is what the Wild Hawg ingredient line is doing. Nobody needs to read it closely. Its position, density, technical cadence, and relationship to the main mark cast small votes. The visual compiler returns:

established, potent, legitimate energy product

without necessarily returning:

this reminds me of Monster.

So yes—the important discovery is bigger than peripheral vision:

Attention is a movable fovea. Every modality has a field. Conscious experience is the interface between them.